+# VDMOS compatibility and known defects
+
+Candidate 35 repairs recognition of three-terminal VDMOS cards such as
+`M1 drain gate source POWER M=2`. It follows ngspice 47's `INP2M` rule that
+model-name lookup begins after three terminals; only VDMOS may omit the fourth
+terminal. Four/five-terminal forms remain accepted, and six-terminal VDMOS
+cards now raise a parse error. MOSFET model references also resolve without
+regard to case and use the declaration's shared model card.
+
+The eight-cell `vdmos_terminal_forms.cir` regression checks N/P polarity,
+three-terminal default and `M=2` instances, four/five-terminal controls,
+mixed-case model references and whitespace around instance assignments.
+It checks actual VDMOS device classes, all public operating-point observables
+against ngspice 47, nonzero drain currents and the expected multiplier scaling.
+The standard comparison tolerance is unchanged. The thermal terminals in these
+controls are grounded; they do not establish self-heating support.
+
+All four primary/driven fixtures enclosing SGN20N40L and VT6K1 now match the
+reference. A separate device inspection confirms one VDMOS instance for
+SGN20N40L and two for VT6K1 in both variants. These results repair the missing
+instances identified in checkpoint 34. They do not certify the models under
+arbitrary bias, temperature or analysis settings.
+
+## Stronger diagnostics and remaining blockers
+
+VT6K1 still emits warnings that `TEMP` defaults to zero. A diagnostic with
+drain voltage 2 V, gate voltages 0.7/1.5 V and temperatures 0/27/60 °C fails
+all six reference comparisons. At 27 °C and gate voltage 0.7 V, native drain
+supply current is approximately -0.972669 mA, versus -2.274588 mA in ngspice 47.
+This is a real unresolved correctness defect despite the minimal corpus matches;
+the temperature warning alone does not isolate every cause of the discrepancy.
+
+A separate global-model control compares a `Vto` expression containing `TEMP`
+with its literal value. The reference expression and literal results agree at
+all three temperatures. Native global model parsing omits the expression's
+`Vto` parameter and uses the default threshold; at 27 °C its expression cell
+conducts about 2 pA instead of 2.174185 mA. The literal controls agree with the
+reference. A four-terminal control gives identical native values in candidates
+34 and 35, proving this expression defect predates the terminal-parser fix.
+
+Subcircuit model scope also remains a blocker. A three-terminal VDMOS referencing
+a local model works, but one referencing a global model fails: subcircuit
+expansion still treats the model token as a fourth node. The four-terminal
+global-model control and both local-model controls match the reference.
+
+| Area | Current status |
+|---|---|
+| DC and IV sweep | Existing N/P operating-point and N-channel sweep tests pass, together with the new terminal-layout regression. |
+| AC and noise | Explicitly rejected with `SimulationError`; existing tests verify the rejection, including `no_throw`. |
+| Model expressions and temperature-dependent parameters | Known correctness defects described above; not validated by the four passing corpus fixtures. |
+| Model scope in subcircuits | Three-terminal references to global models remain a known parser defect. |
+| Thermal and instance options | The adapter is isothermal. `TEMP`, `DTEMP`, `IC`, `OFF`, thermal behavior and operating-point queries need further coverage and correction where ignored. |
+| General transient behavior | Not certified by these DC/parser checks. |
+
+Candidate 35 passes 1,251/1,252 Release C++ tests, all 393 Python/tooling tests,
+51 parser/BJT sanitizer tests and all eight VDMOS sanitizer tests. Leak detection
+is enabled in those focused sanitizer groups. RFF70N06 remains the C++ failure.
+All 34 comprehensive, five THS4131 and one TLV benchmark accuracy gates pass;
+no new performance timings were collected. The [progress tracker](joss-progress.md)
+links the full corpus audit and preserved diagnostic outputs.
